1
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:12,102
Beyond a green star, close to a white
sun, lived a people called the Steelypips.

2
00:00:12,142 --> 00:00:16,570
They had no quarrels and no lessons.
They lived on, in, and under one machine.

3
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:19,685
It was a fine machine
of gears and springs.

4
00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:22,294
They had built it from
their own saved atoms.

5
00:00:23,194 --> 00:00:26,440
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: We made it
ourselves, so there is nothing in it to fix.

6
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:29,651
One day a comet came by, in a lace bonnet,

7
00:00:29,691 --> 00:00:32,114
with four long tails
and a smell of almonds.

8
00:00:32,154 --> 00:00:35,232
She said she would burn them
to the ground, as a start.

9
00:00:35,832 --> 00:00:38,782
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: One moment,
madam. This machine has no faults.

10
00:00:38,822 --> 00:00:40,236
Please leave, or you will be sorry.

11
00:00:41,036 --> 00:00:45,019
She filled the sky with fire, until
their old moon curled up at both tips.

12
00:00:45,059 --> 00:00:49,463
So the Steelypips wrapped their strongest
fields around it, and threw the switch.

13
00:00:51,863 --> 00:00:56,137
The sky cleared with a bang. All that
was left of the comet was a bit of ash.

14
00:00:56,177 --> 00:00:58,530
The Steelypips went back to being pleased.

15
00:01:00,930 --> 00:01:05,488
Some time later, something came down
from the sky. Nobody knew what it was.

16
00:01:05,528 --> 00:01:10,248
It landed on the highest peak, very
heavily, and nobody wanted a second look.

17
00:01:10,948 --> 00:01:13,688
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: Pardon
us, but this is a very good machine.

18
00:01:13,728 --> 00:01:14,433
Please move along.

19
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:18,882
It did not move. It did not
attack either. It just stayed.

20
00:01:19,682 --> 00:01:23,342
So, to save money, they sent a
small scarechrome to frighten it.

21
00:01:23,842 --> 00:01:25,842
THE SCARECHROME: Fear me.
I am terrible. Fear me.

22
00:01:26,642 --> 00:01:30,525
It hissed and spat and frightened
itself a little. The thing stayed.

23
00:01:30,565 --> 00:01:33,853
It tried a second frequency,
but its heart was not in it.

24
00:01:36,053 --> 00:01:38,065
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON:
Something heavier, then.

25
00:01:38,105 --> 00:01:40,788
Hydraulic, with plenty of muscle,
and a scarechrome on top for luck.

26
00:01:41,488 --> 00:01:45,875
The great engine rolled out. It wound
itself up, and counted down from four.

27
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:52,551
Oil bubbled and gears
rattled. Not a scratch.

28
00:01:53,351 --> 00:01:55,392
The Steelypips held a council of war.

29
00:01:55,432 --> 00:01:59,160
They built a machine to build a
machine to build a machine so large

30
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:03,025
that the nearest stars stepped back,
with a servospook at its heart.

31
00:02:03,625 --> 00:02:06,643
THE SERVOSPOOK: I am the
servospook. I mean business.

32
00:02:07,243 --> 00:02:09,512
It gathered all its strength, and let go.

33
00:02:18,912 --> 00:02:22,387
When the dark cleared, there was only
scrap metal, ticking as it cooled.

34
00:02:23,687 --> 00:02:28,124
Each weapon had been bigger than the
last. The thing was the same size.

35
00:02:30,124 --> 00:02:32,325
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON:
Then we must be patient.

36
00:02:32,365 --> 00:02:35,079
We will send a cyberivy, to creep
up quietly and take its time.

37
00:02:35,879 --> 00:02:38,844
The ivy crept up as if it
had nothing on its mind.

38
00:02:38,884 --> 00:02:42,450
It sent out a root, then another,
and closed in from behind.

39
00:02:42,490 --> 00:02:46,457
It went exactly as planned, and
when it was over, it was not over.

40
00:02:47,157 --> 00:02:49,727
They made nets and
glues, lassos and screws,

41
00:02:49,767 --> 00:02:52,811
traps and walls, and turned
the machine upside down.

42
00:02:52,851 --> 00:02:53,741
Nothing helped.

43
00:02:55,241 --> 00:02:57,804
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: This
has never happened to us before.

44
00:02:57,844 --> 00:02:59,486
We do not know what to do with our hands.

45
00:03:01,886 --> 00:03:05,117
Then someone came along on
a vehicle nobody could name.

46
00:03:05,157 --> 00:03:08,745
It had a horse’s head, one
bicycle wheel and a rocket’s tail.

47
00:03:08,785 --> 00:03:11,462
The rider was Trurl,
out on one of his trips.

48
00:03:12,062 --> 00:03:15,927
THE STEELYPIPS: We tried
everything. It is still there.

49
00:03:16,627 --> 00:03:19,187
TRURL: Did you try anything
that was not a weapon?

50
00:03:19,687 --> 00:03:22,474
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: What else is
there? We have gears, springs, and force.

51
00:03:23,174 --> 00:03:27,028
TRURL: I can stay a little while,
as an adviser. Is that agreeable?

52
00:03:27,528 --> 00:03:30,816
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: Of course!
Shall we bring hammers, artillery, dynamite?

53
00:03:30,856 --> 00:03:32,700
Coffee or tea? The vending
machine makes both.

54
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:36,210
TRURL: Coffee. Not for me,
for the job. The rest, no.

55
00:03:37,210 --> 00:03:39,889
Force has failed, so we will use forms.

56
00:03:40,689 --> 00:03:44,321
I need paper and ink, stamps
and seals, a teller’s window,

57
00:03:44,361 --> 00:03:47,233
a zinc spoon, a saucer,
coffee, and a mailman.

58
00:03:47,273 --> 00:03:49,046
And something to write with.

59
00:03:49,446 --> 00:03:50,936
THE STEELYPIPS: We will fetch it!

60
00:03:53,136 --> 00:03:55,984
Trurl sat down, and
dictated the first notice.

61
00:03:56,584 --> 00:04:01,047
TRURL: Regarding the obstruction
of these premises, under Form Four,

62
00:04:01,087 --> 00:04:05,880
subsection Q: all services to the
occupant are withdrawn, pending review.

63
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:09,165
Appeals must be in writing,
on the correct forms.

64
00:04:09,865 --> 00:04:13,990
He sealed it, stamped it, entered it in
the ledger, and checked the register.

65
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:15,599
Then he sent for the mailman.

66
00:04:16,299 --> 00:04:18,718
The mailman took it away. They waited.

67
00:04:24,318 --> 00:04:25,439
TRURL: Was it delivered?

68
00:04:25,839 --> 00:04:26,734
THE MAILMAN: It was.

69
00:04:27,134 --> 00:04:28,094
TRURL: And the receipt?

70
00:04:28,494 --> 00:04:30,958
THE MAILMAN: Signed, on this
line. And here is its appeal.

71
00:04:32,558 --> 00:04:37,186
TRURL: Once it took delivery of the first
notice, it belonged to the paperwork.

72
00:04:38,086 --> 00:04:40,580
Without reading the
appeal, he sent it back.

73
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:43,516
Across the front he wrote,
in a hurry, wrong forms,

74
00:04:43,556 --> 00:04:46,032
and signed with a scrawl
no one could read.

75
00:04:46,632 --> 00:04:47,650
TRURL: Now, to work.

76
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:53,125
The mailman ran all day.
Trurl stamped and filed.

77
00:04:53,165 --> 00:04:58,182
The typewriter chattered, and an office grew
out of nothing: stamps and rubber bands,

78
00:04:58,222 --> 00:05:02,505
paper clips, pigeonholes, filing
cabinets, signs, coffee rings, cobwebs.

79
00:05:03,005 --> 00:05:06,517
Notices went out, then
reminders, then final demands.

80
00:05:12,517 --> 00:05:14,786
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: It
is smaller. It really is smaller.

81
00:05:15,986 --> 00:05:19,606
TRURL: Next, please. The window
is closed. Come back in an hour.

82
00:05:21,806 --> 00:05:25,798
The coffee went cold. An official
was offered a bribe, and filed it.

83
00:05:25,838 --> 00:05:29,396
New cabinets arrived, and cobwebs
reached from wall to wall.

84
00:05:32,396 --> 00:05:36,741
The typewriter typed an order that the
occupant must leave, and may not appeal.

85
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:55,457
Then Trurl stood up and threw
it all out into deep space:

86
00:05:55,497 --> 00:05:58,988
the desks, the chairs, the stamps,
the seals, the pigeonholes.

87
00:05:59,028 --> 00:06:00,851
Only the vending machine stayed.

88
00:06:01,351 --> 00:06:03,559
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: What are
you doing? We had grown used to it!

89
00:06:04,159 --> 00:06:06,052
TRURL: Take a look instead, friends.

90
00:06:07,052 --> 00:06:08,980
They looked, and the thing was gone.

91
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:12,425
It had shrunk and shrunk, until
they needed a magnifying glass.

92
00:06:12,465 --> 00:06:16,457
All they could find was one small damp
spot, where something had dripped.

93
00:06:17,057 --> 00:06:19,809
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: But why throw
out the papers, and pour out the coffee?

94
00:06:20,709 --> 00:06:23,772
TRURL: Throw it all out, or
the office will start on you.

95
00:06:24,672 --> 00:06:28,999
He nodded to them kindly, climbed onto
his odd vehicle, and flew away, smiling.

